        %p.tutorial-number= tut_card_s3t_number
        %h1= s3t_h1
        %p.sub-lead= s3t_lead

        %h2= s3t_h2_dual
        %p
          YOSHIMITSU is the <strong>Hermetic Shinobi</strong> with two faces — a rock-steady
          CRSF→PWM <em>converter</em> and a pocket <em>flasher</em> — fused into one
          combined firmware. Both faces live on the same board, so one permanent harness
          serves flying <em>and</em> flashing. Two builds are provided, one per board:
        %table
          %tr
            %th Build
            %th Sketch
            %th Face switch
            %th Used for
          %tr
            %td YOSHIMITSU · ESP32-S3
            %td
              %code esp32s3_yoshimitsu
            %td GPIO0 double-tap (BOOT button)
            %td Native USB, every GPIO PWM-capable, up to 8 servo channels
          %tr
            %td YOSHIMITSU · RP2040-Tiny
            %td
              %code rp2040_tiny_yoshimitsu
            %td Buttonless — FLASHER command + RESET tap
            %td UART0/UART1 on fixed pins, onboard WS2812B status LED
        %p
          Both builds live in the repository under
          <code>sketches/esp32s3_yoshimitsu/</code> and
          <code>sketches/rp2040_tiny_yoshimitsu/</code> and compile with the
          standard arduino-esp32 / arduino-pico cores.

        %h2= s3t_h2_yoshimitsu
        %p
          <strong>YOSHIMITSU</strong> — the Hermetic Shinobi — is the recommended firmware: one board,
          two faces. It boots in <strong>FACE&nbsp;I</strong> (converter) and switches to
          <strong>FACE&nbsp;II</strong> (flasher) with a GPIO0 double-tap. The BOOT button is the
          single control surface:
        %table
          %tr
            %th Face
            %th Gesture
            %th Action
          %tr
            %td FACE I (converter)
            %td double-tap
            %td enter FACE II (flasher)
          %tr
            %td FACE II (flasher)
            %td single tap
            %td restart the receiver (run the new firmware)
          %tr
            %td FACE II (flasher)
            %td double-tap
            %td drop the receiver into its bootloader (flash mode)
          %tr
            %td FACE II (flasher)
            %td long-press 2 s
            %td return to FACE I (converter)
        %p
          The wiring is <strong>one permanent harness</strong> — the same six wires serve both
          faces, so you never re-wire between flying and flashing. In FACE&nbsp;II the USB↔UART
          path is a <em>pure transparent bridge</em> (no line parsing), so esptool's binary SLIP
          traffic passes through untouched. Full source:
          <code>sketches/esp32s3_yoshimitsu/esp32s3_yoshimitsu.ino</code>.

        %h2= s3t_h2_conv
        %p
          <strong>FACE&nbsp;I</strong> — the converter — follows the exact procedure described in
          <a href="#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}tutorials/crsf-converter/">Tutorial 06 — CRSF→PWM Converter</a>:
          CRSF at 420&nbsp;000&nbsp;baud on the UART, servos on three free GPIOs,
          a 988–2012&nbsp;µs window mapped from the 172–1811 channel range, and a
          failsafe that centres the servos when no valid frame arrives for 500&nbsp;ms.
        %ul
          %li Wiring recap: RX&nbsp;TX → S3&nbsp;GPIO44, RX&nbsp;RX → S3&nbsp;GPIO43, common GND, shared 5&nbsp;V rail.
          %li Servos: GPIO1 (left wing), GPIO2 (right wing), GPIO3 (crest rudder) — all overrideable in the pin-map block at the top of the sketch.
          %li Full, CRC-checked source: <code>sketches/esp32s3_yoshimitsu/esp32s3_yoshimitsu.ino</code>
        %p
          If your receiver already speaks PWM (a 5&nbsp;ch or 7&nbsp;ch board), you do not need
          FACE&nbsp;I at all — FACE&nbsp;II below is then your only reason to own the S3.

        %h2= s3t_h2_flasher
        %p
          <strong>The problem:</strong> EP2-class receivers (HappyModel EP2, EP1, EPW6, …) expose
          CRSF on castellated pads and carry a tiny BOOT pad. Using an FTDI adapter means holding
          a probe against two 1&nbsp;mm pads <em>while</em> power-cycling the receiver — awkward,
          and you re-solder or re-clip every time the firmware needs an update.
        %p
          <strong>The solution:</strong> let the ESP32-S3 be the <em>flasher</em>. One GPIO holds the
          receiver's BOOT pad low; a small P-MOSFET switches the receiver's 3.3&nbsp;V rail under
          software control; the USB port becomes the serial port. Press one button on the S3 and it
          performs the exact power-cycle dance that drops an ESP8285 into its ROM bootloader — then
          esptool writes straight through the bridge. No FTDI, no probes, no repeated soldering:
          solder the receiver's pads <em>once</em> to a short pigtail and reuse it forever.
        %p Parts list:
        %table
          %tr
            %th Part
            %th Typical part number
            %th Cost
          %tr
            %td ESP32-S3 micro board (native USB)
            %td Waveshare ESP32-S3-Tiny / -Micro / -Nano
            %td ~6 USD
          %tr
            %td P-channel logic-level MOSFET
            %td AO3401 (SOT-23) on a small breakout
            %td ~1 USD
          %tr
            %td Resistor 10&nbsp;kΩ (gate pull-up)
            %td —
            %td &lt;0.10 USD
          %tr
            %td Dupont / pigtail wires + heat-shrink
            %td —
            %td ~1 USD
          %tr
            %td ELRS receiver, pads pigtailed once
            %td EP2 / EP1 / EPW6 (SX1280 + ESP8285)
            %td from ~12 USD
        %p.hw-note
          The bridge works with every ESP8285/SX1280 ELRS receiver that exposes TX, RX, 3V3,
          GND and a BOOT pad — the overwhelming majority of the <em>concrete boards</em> in the
          <a href="#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}hardware/">hardware table</a>. It also flashes the S3
          itself, of course: BOOT button + USB is all that needs there.

        %h2= s3t_h2_wiring
        %p
          Wire the jig with the S3 unpowered. The MOSFET switches the receiver's 3.3&nbsp;V rail:
          source ← S3&nbsp;3V3, drain → EP2&nbsp;3V3, gate ← S3&nbsp;GPIO10 with a 10&nbsp;kΩ pull-up
          to 3V3 (gate high = MOSFET off). GPIO9 drives the BOOT pad.
        %table
          %tr
            %th EP2 / RX pad
            %th ESP32-S3 pin
            %th Function
          %tr
            %td BOOT (RX GPIO0)
            %td GPIO9
            %td Boot-hold line (low = enter bootloader)
          %tr
            %td 3V3
            %td MOSFET drain
            %td Switched rail (source ← S3 3V3, gate ← GPIO10)
          %tr
            %td TX (CRSF out)
            %td GPIO18 (U1RX)
            %td RX ← TX (cross)
          %tr
            %td RX (CRSF in)
            %td GPIO17 (U1TX)
            %td TX → RX (cross)
          %tr
            %td GND
            %td GND
            %td Common ground (mandatory)
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              S3 3V3   ──► S ── P-MOSFET (AO3401) ── D ──► EP2 3V3
              S3 GPIO10 ──► gate         (10kΩ pull-up to 3V3; LOW = ON)
              S3 GPIO9  ──► EP2 BOOT pad (asserted LOW during power-up)
              S3 GPIO18 ◄── EP2 TX       (U1 RX)
              S3 GPIO17 ──► EP2 RX       (U1 TX)
              S3 GND     ──► EP2 GND
        %aside.waveform-caution
          %strong Power polarity & voltage
          — feed the receiver <strong>3.3&nbsp;V only</strong>, never 5&nbsp;V, while it is on the
          jig. Verify the MOSFET with a voltmeter before connecting a receiver: with GPIO10 HIGH the
          drain must read 0&nbsp;V; with GPIO10 LOW it must read 3.3&nbsp;V.

        %h2= s3t_h2_mode
        %p
          FACE&nbsp;II boots into <em>transparent passthrough</em> (USB ↔ UART at
          115&nbsp;200&nbsp;baud). To enter <strong>flash mode</strong>, press the S3's BOOT button
          twice within one second — or send the command <code>FLASHER</code> over the USB serial. The
          sketch then:
        %ol
          %li pulls GPIO9 low (BOOT pad asserted),
          %li switches the MOSFET off for 120&nbsp;ms (receiver power off),
          %li switches it back on and waits 800&nbsp;ms (receiver powers up inside its bootloader),
          %li releases GPIO9 — the RX now sits in the ROM bootloader, waiting for esptool,
          %li resumes USB↔UART passthrough so esptool can talk to it.
        %p
          Because the bridge cannot drive the classic DTR/RTS auto-reset lines, always flash with
          <code>--before no_reset</code> — the power-cycle dance above <em>is</em> the reset.
          After flashing, a <strong>single</strong> BOOT press power-cycles the receiver again so
          it boots the new firmware; two presses within the window would merely re-enter flash mode.

        %h2= s3t_h2_esptool
        %p
          Build the firmware (or fetch a release), then run esptool against the S3's USB serial
          port. On macOS the port shows up as <code>/dev/cu.usbmodem*</code>:
        %pre
          %code
            :preserve
              # 1. Build PteronautOS (from the repository root)
              pio run -e PteronautOS_ESP8285_2400_RX

              # 2. Drop the RX into flash mode via the S3 (BOOT twice), then:
              python3 -m esptool --chip esp8285 \
                --port /dev/cu.usbmodemXXXX --baud 115200 \
                --before no_reset write_flash \
                --flash_mode dout --flash_size 1MB --flash_freq 40m \
                0x0 .pio/build/PteronautOS_ESP8285_2400_RX/firmware.bin

              # 3. Restart the RX once more (single BOOT press) to run the new firmware.
        %p
          The flash settings (dout, 1&nbsp;MB, 40&nbsp;MHz) match the stock ESP8285 ELRS target —
          never use qio or a larger size. After flashing, the very same USB port shows the
          receiver's boot log: open it at 115&nbsp;200&nbsp;baud and watch for the PteronautOS banner
          and, once a transmitter binds, the CRSF link-up messages. The bridge doubles as your
          field console for live telemetry.

        %h2= s3t_h2_verify
        %ul
          %li Flash mode: BOOT twice must power-cycle the RX and leave it waiting — the 3.3&nbsp;V dips for ~120&nbsp;ms.
          %li esptool reports <code>Chip is ESP8285</code> and finishes with <code>Hash of data verified</code>.
          %li The boot log shows the PteronautOS banner and, with the radio on, a link-up message.
          %li Wiggle the sticks: servos track smoothly; switch the radio off: wings centre to 1500&nbsp;µs within a second.
          %li If the RX never enters the bootloader, check the BOOT-pad polarity (active low) and the MOSFET switching with the voltmeter test above.
        %p
          Once flashed, the same receiver flies — no FTDI left behind.

        %h2= s3t_h2_rp2040
        %p
          Prefer the <strong>Waveshare RP2040-Tiny</strong> (~5&nbsp;USD) — or its twin, the
          RP2040-Zero, which shares the exact same pinout? The same two faces run on the RP2040 with
          <strong>no readable button at all</strong>: the adapter's BOOT/RESET taps only drive
          BOOTSEL/RUN, so YOSHIMITSU-RP2040 is driven entirely over the USB serial port.
        %ul
          %li <code>FLASHER</code> — enter FACE&nbsp;II and drop the receiver into its ROM bootloader (MOSFET power-cycle dance), then bridge USB ↔ UART for esptool.
          %li <code>STATUS</code> — print the active pin map and the self-test report.
          %li <code>HELP</code> — list every command.
        %p
          To flash the RP2040-Tiny itself, hold <code>BOOT</code> while power-cycling (it mounts as a
          mass-storage drive), drop the <code>.uf2</code> on it, then press <code>RESET</code> once —
          the board reboots into FACE&nbsp;I running the new firmware. Configure once, flash once,
          never touch again.

        %h2= s3t_h2_rp2040_pins
        %table
          %tr
            %th Function
            %th RP2040-Tiny pin
            %th Note
          %tr
            %td CRSF RX ↔ receiver TX
            %td GPIO1 (UART0 RX)
            %td 420&nbsp;000&nbsp;baud
          %tr
            %td CRSF TX (unused, wired for completeness)
            %td GPIO0 (UART0 TX)
            %td Cross: TX → RX
          %tr
            %td Bridge RX ↔ receiver TX
            %td GPIO9 (UART1 RX)
            %td 115&nbsp;200&nbsp;baud
          %tr
            %td Bridge TX → receiver RX
            %td GPIO8 (UART1 TX)
            %td Cross: TX → RX
          %tr
            %td Servo left wing
            %td GPIO2
            %td PWM
          %tr
            %td Servo right wing
            %td GPIO3
            %td PWM
          %tr
            %td Servo crest rudder
            %td GPIO4
            %td PWM
          %tr
            %td RX BOOT (receiver GPIO0, active low)
            %td GPIO5
            %td Low = enter bootloader
          %tr
            %td RX PWR (P-MOSFET gate, low = powered)
            %td GPIO6
            %td Switched 3.3&nbsp;V rail
          %tr
            %td Status LED
            %td GPIO16
            %td WS2812B
        %p
          The complete pin map is an editable block at the top of
          <code>sketches/rp2040_tiny_yoshimitsu/rp2040_tiny_yoshimitsu.ino</code> — every pin is
          validated at compile time, so a collision or an invalid UART number refuses to build
          rather than misbehaving in the air.
